    "description": "You are given an integer array nums and two integers limit and goal. The array nums has an interesting property that abs(nums[i]) <= limit.\nReturn the minimum number of elements you need to add to make the sum of the array equal to goal. The array must maintain its property that abs(nums[i]) <= limit.\nNote that abs(x) equals x if x >= 0, and -x otherwise.\n<div class=\"constraints-wrapper\"><h3 class=\"constraint-header\">Constraints</h3><ul class=\"constraint-list\"><li>1 &lt;= nums.length &lt;= 105</li><li>1 &lt;= limit &lt;= 106</li><li>-limit &lt;= nums[i] &lt;= limit</li><li>-109 &lt;= goal &lt;= 109</li></ul></div>\n            <div class=\"example-wrapper\">\n              <h3 class=\"example-header\">Example 1</h3>\n              <div class=\"example-block\"><br/><span class=\"ex-label input\">Input</span> nums = [1,-1,1], limit = 3, goal = -4\n<br/><span class=\"ex-label output\">Output</span> 2\n<br/><span class=\"ex-label explain\">Explanation</span> You can add -2 and -3, then the sum of the array will be 1 - 1 + 1 - 2 - 3 = -4.</div>\n            </div>\n          \n            <div class=\"example-wrapper\">\n              <h3 class=\"example-header\">Example 2</h3>\n              <div class=\"example-block\"><br/><span class=\"ex-label input\">Input</span> nums = [1,-10,9,1], limit = 100, goal = 0\n<br/><span class=\"ex-label output\">Output</span> 1</div>\n            </div>\n          ",
